%I A363836 #32 Nov 01 2026 22:49:30
%S A363836 39,587,124499
%N A363836 a(n) = smallest number m such that digitsum(m^n) < digitsum(m), or -1 if no such m exists.
%C A363836 a(n) > 0 when n is even (see MSE link).
%C A363836 10^12 < a(5) <= 1000000000000001999999999999999000000000000002000000000000001. - _Max Alekseyev_, Jun 17 2026
%C A363836 a(n) > 0 for all n > 1. This follows from a theorem of Hare, Laishram and Stoll: for any polynomial p(x) with integer coefficients, degree greater than 1, and positive leading coefficient, and any integer base q > 1, lim inf_{m -> oo} s_q(p(m)) / s_q(m) = 0, where s_q(N) denotes the sum of base-q digits of N. Taking p(x) = x^n implies there are infinitely many m with digitsum(m^n) < digitsum(m), so the minimal such m is well-defined. - _Lingling Tong_, Oct 26 2026
%H A363836 Kevin G. Hare, Shanta Laishram, and Thomas Stoll, <a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/41059195">Stolarsky's conjecture and the sum of digits of polynomial values</a>, Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. 139 (2011), 39-49. Also on <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.4169">arxiv</a>. arXiv:1001.4169 [math.NT], 2010.
%H A363836 Jeppe Stig Nielsen et al., <a href="https://math.stackexchange.com/q/1401871">Integers n for which the digit sum of n exceeds the digit sum of n^5</a>, Math StackExchange, 2015.
%e A363836 a(2) = A064399(1).
%e A363836 a(3) = A064209(1).
%e A363836 a(4) = A261439(1).
